// `JsPathSegment`'s home, and its only reason for being a module of its own:
// `spec/ffi.md` section 12's extension makes all five constructors
// qualified-only — `JsPathSegment.Index(3)`, never a bare `Index` — and a
// qualified constructor is spelled through the module that declares it (Modules
// section 3.3, the `Geo.Circle(r)` mechanism). The union sits here so that home
// has the name the spec writes, exactly as `JsKind.hex` does for `JsKind`.
//
// The names are the whole argument. `Field`, `Index`, `MapKey`, `MapValue` and
// `SetElement` are ordinary user vocabulary, and a prelude that spent them bare
// would stand in the way of every program that wanted one of them for itself.
// Path vocabulary is overwhelmingly *matched* and rarely constructed, so the
// qualified spelling costs little exactly where it is used.

(** Where inside a value a checked conversion failed. Segments read
    root-outward, so the `name` field of the 3rd element of the value under the
    2nd map key is `[JsPathSegment.MapKey(2), JsPathSegment.Index(3),
    JsPathSegment.Field("name")]`.

    `Index` and the three entry positions are 1-based, like every other index in
    the language: a path is a Hexagon diagnostic, not a JavaScript offset. A
    foreign key cannot itself be carried here, so a collection entry is named by
    its position in the source's own iteration order.

    Every constructor is reached qualified: `JsPathSegment.Field(name)`,
    `JsPathSegment.Index(index)`, and so on, in expressions and in patterns
    alike. *)
export union JsPathSegment =
    | Field(name: String)
    | Index(index: Int)
    | MapKey(position: Int)
    | MapValue(position: Int)
    | SetElement(position: Int)
